WEBVTT

00:00:00.344 --> 00:00:01.536
Last video ended here.

00:00:02.362 --> 00:00:04.966
This curve, f of x equals x cubed

00:00:05.026 --> 00:00:07.691
minus three x, has a local maximum at

00:00:07.871 --> 00:00:08.893
minus one, two.

00:00:09.855 --> 00:00:11.822
Picture it as a stretch of roller coaster

00:00:11.863 --> 00:00:14.472
track, with f the height in metres above

00:00:14.494 --> 00:00:15.015
the platform.

00:00:15.738 --> 00:00:18.046
The stretch runs from x equals minus two

00:00:18.128 --> 00:00:20.717
to x equals three, with both ends included.

00:00:21.622 --> 00:00:23.946
Near minus one there's a small hump, and

00:00:24.027 --> 00:00:25.711
nothing nearby is higher than two.

00:00:26.353 --> 00:00:27.541
But ride on to the end of the

00:00:27.582 --> 00:00:29.072
stretch, up the big climb.

00:00:29.456 --> 00:00:30.502
At x equals three,

00:00:31.088 --> 00:00:32.033
f is eighteen.

00:00:32.617 --> 00:00:35.231
So a local maximum isn't always the largest

00:00:35.293 --> 00:00:35.593
value.

00:00:36.338 --> 00:00:38.305
How do we find the largest and smallest

00:00:38.347 --> 00:00:39.550
values on a whole interval,

00:00:40.094 --> 00:00:41.358
and make sure we haven't missed one?

00:00:42.029 --> 00:00:44.559
The largest value over the whole domain is

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:46.186
called the global maximum.

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:47.269
A point

00:00:47.871 --> 00:00:50.351
a, f of a, is a global maximum

00:00:50.672 --> 00:00:52.352
if f of x is less than or

00:00:52.493 --> 00:00:54.773
equal to f of a, for every x

00:00:54.955 --> 00:00:55.514
in the domain.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:58.552
Picture a horizontal line at that height.

00:00:59.156 --> 00:01:01.123
For a global maximum, no part of the

00:01:01.164 --> 00:01:02.107
graph pokes above it.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:04.817
Put the line at two, the local maximum,

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:07.327
and the curve pokes through near the right-hand

00:01:07.428 --> 00:01:07.568
end.

00:01:08.211 --> 00:01:10.297
Put it at eighteen, and the whole graph

00:01:10.438 --> 00:01:11.701
sits on or below it.

00:01:12.445 --> 00:01:14.512
A global minimum is the same idea with

00:01:14.553 --> 00:01:15.034
a floor.

00:01:15.537 --> 00:01:17.144
No part of the graph dips below it.

00:01:17.789 --> 00:01:19.336
So where can the highest point be?

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.565
Suppose it's inside the interval, where the curve

00:01:22.606 --> 00:01:23.007
is smooth.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.604
Then its tangent must be flat.

00:01:26.491 --> 00:01:28.716
If the tangent sloped either way, you could

00:01:28.837 --> 00:01:30.700
step a little uphill and find a higher

00:01:30.761 --> 00:01:31.001
point.

00:01:31.685 --> 00:01:34.061
So inside, look at stationary points.

00:01:34.826 --> 00:01:36.914
But at an end, the curve just stops.

00:01:37.598 --> 00:01:39.405
It doesn't need to be flat, because the

00:01:39.607 --> 00:01:41.375
uphill direction leaves the domain.

00:01:42.099 --> 00:01:44.384
And at a sharp corner, there's no single

00:01:44.465 --> 00:01:48.053
tangent, so f prime doesn't exist there, and

00:01:48.114 --> 00:01:50.439
solving f prime equals zero can't find it.

00:01:51.322 --> 00:01:52.864
On a closed interval where the curve is

00:01:53.005 --> 00:01:56.690
unbroken, the highest and lowest values always exist,

00:01:57.151 --> 00:01:58.512
and each one is at one of these

00:01:58.573 --> 00:01:59.033
candidates.

00:01:59.776 --> 00:02:02.103
If the curve breaks somewhere, examine that point

00:02:02.145 --> 00:02:02.344
too.

00:02:03.103 --> 00:02:04.337
That gives a short method.

00:02:04.962 --> 00:02:08.050
List the candidates: stationary points inside the interval,

00:02:08.551 --> 00:02:11.318
any interior points where f prime doesn't exist,

00:02:11.739 --> 00:02:12.722
and both endpoints.

00:02:13.303 --> 00:02:15.632
Then work out f at every one, and

00:02:15.672 --> 00:02:16.053
compare.

00:02:16.795 --> 00:02:19.180
Here, f prime of x is three, times

00:02:19.401 --> 00:02:21.606
x minus one, times x plus one.

00:02:22.088 --> 00:02:24.293
So the stationary points are minus one and

00:02:24.514 --> 00:02:25.656
one, both inside.

00:02:26.399 --> 00:02:28.985
The endpoints are minus two and three, and

00:02:29.146 --> 00:02:30.689
f prime exists everywhere.

00:02:31.352 --> 00:02:34.278
The values are minus two, two, minus two

00:02:34.299 --> 00:02:35.220
and eighteen.

00:02:35.941 --> 00:02:38.707
The largest is eighteen, so the global maximum

00:02:38.828 --> 00:02:40.771
is eighteen, at x equals three.

00:02:41.453 --> 00:02:43.757
The smallest is minus two, and it happens

00:02:43.838 --> 00:02:46.582
twice, at x equals minus two and x

00:02:46.723 --> 00:02:47.343
equals one.

00:02:48.045 --> 00:02:49.987
On the coaster, the highest point of this

00:02:50.028 --> 00:02:51.870
stretch is the top of the climb, not

00:02:51.911 --> 00:02:54.374
the hump, and the lowest point comes twice,

00:02:54.755 --> 00:02:56.317
at the start and in the dip.

00:02:57.042 --> 00:02:59.035
Notice we never had to classify anything.

00:02:59.459 --> 00:03:00.955
Comparing the values settles it.

00:03:01.563 --> 00:03:02.677
Now a worked example.

00:03:03.262 --> 00:03:05.670
Find the global maximum and minimum of f

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:08.097
of x equals x plus four over x,

00:03:08.458 --> 00:03:10.043
for x from a half to four.

00:03:10.786 --> 00:03:11.066
First,

00:03:11.708 --> 00:03:13.954
f prime of x is one minus four

00:03:14.115 --> 00:03:14.897
over x squared.

00:03:15.579 --> 00:03:17.808
Setting that to zero gives x squared equals

00:03:17.928 --> 00:03:20.820
four, so x is two or minus two.

00:03:21.525 --> 00:03:23.458
Minus two isn't in the interval, so reject

00:03:23.479 --> 00:03:23.578
it.

00:03:24.304 --> 00:03:26.965
And f isn't defined at zero, but zero

00:03:27.086 --> 00:03:28.206
isn't in the interval either,

00:03:28.727 --> 00:03:30.727
so the curve is unbroken here, with no

00:03:30.788 --> 00:03:31.148
corners.

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:34.438
The candidates are a half, two and four.

00:03:35.422 --> 00:03:37.150
Now evaluate f at each one.

00:03:38.033 --> 00:03:40.278
f of a half is a half plus

00:03:40.359 --> 00:03:41.943
eight, which is eight and a half.

00:03:42.845 --> 00:03:44.953
f of two is two plus two,

00:03:45.454 --> 00:03:45.675
four.

00:03:46.539 --> 00:03:48.652
f of four is four plus one,

00:03:49.175 --> 00:03:49.497
five.

00:03:50.369 --> 00:03:50.819
Compare them.

00:03:51.421 --> 00:03:54.028
The global minimum is four, at the stationary

00:03:54.068 --> 00:03:55.572
point x equals two.

00:03:56.274 --> 00:03:58.598
The global maximum is eight and a half,

00:03:58.939 --> 00:04:01.503
at the endpoint x equals a half, where

00:04:01.524 --> 00:04:02.946
the tangent isn't flat at all.

00:04:03.608 --> 00:04:06.356
Solving f prime equals zero alone would have

00:04:06.437 --> 00:04:07.238
missed it completely.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:09.685
The same method works for an exponential.

00:04:10.329 --> 00:04:12.354
Find the greatest and least values of g

00:04:12.636 --> 00:04:15.203
of x equals x e to the minus

00:04:15.404 --> 00:04:17.549
x, for x from zero to three.

00:04:18.252 --> 00:04:19.935
By the product rule, g prime of x

00:04:20.137 --> 00:04:22.260
is e to the minus x, times one

00:04:22.301 --> 00:04:22.862
minus x.

00:04:23.605 --> 00:04:25.332
e to the minus x is never zero,

00:04:25.534 --> 00:04:27.884
so the only stationary point is x equals

00:04:28.106 --> 00:04:28.205
one.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:31.246
The candidates are zero, one and three.

00:04:31.994 --> 00:04:33.104
g of zero is zero.

00:04:33.549 --> 00:04:35.735
g of one is one over e, about

00:04:35.976 --> 00:04:36.918
point three six eight.

00:04:37.501 --> 00:04:40.232
g of three is three over e cubed,

00:04:40.595 --> 00:04:42.061
about point one four nine.

00:04:42.764 --> 00:04:44.589
So the greatest value is one over e,

00:04:44.831 --> 00:04:46.877
at x equals one, and the least is

00:04:46.918 --> 00:04:49.425
zero, at the endpoint x equals zero.

00:04:50.208 --> 00:04:53.037
Flats, ends and corners, then compare.

00:04:53.782 --> 00:04:56.518
Flats are the stationary points inside the interval.

00:04:57.365 --> 00:04:58.920
Ends are the two endpoints.

00:04:59.626 --> 00:05:02.472
Include every interior point where f prime doesn't

00:05:02.513 --> 00:05:03.856
exist, such as a corner.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:07.788
Then compare the values of f itself, not

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:08.450
f prime.

00:05:09.133 --> 00:05:11.099
The list is complete on a closed interval

00:05:11.161 --> 00:05:12.645
where the curve is unbroken.

00:05:15.294 --> 00:05:15.783
Your turn.

00:05:16.188 --> 00:05:18.909
Find the global maximum and minimum values of

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:22.190
f of x equals x cubed minus twelve

00:05:22.311 --> 00:05:24.612
x, for x from zero to three,

00:05:25.133 --> 00:05:26.113
and say where they occur.

00:05:26.956 --> 00:05:27.842
Pause here to answer.

00:05:38.615 --> 00:05:40.898
f prime of x is three, times x

00:05:40.939 --> 00:05:43.242
minus two, times x plus two,

00:05:43.784 --> 00:05:46.107
which is zero at two and minus two.

00:05:46.812 --> 00:05:48.864
Minus two isn't in the interval, so reject

00:05:48.926 --> 00:05:49.005
it.

00:05:49.851 --> 00:05:52.813
The candidates are zero, two and three, giving

00:05:52.874 --> 00:05:55.295
zero, minus sixteen and minus nine.

00:05:56.036 --> 00:05:58.440
So the global maximum is zero, at x

00:05:58.481 --> 00:06:00.986
equals zero, and the global minimum is minus

00:06:01.027 --> 00:06:02.749
sixteen, at x equals two.

00:06:03.474 --> 00:06:05.431
Sixteen, from minus two, doesn't count.

00:06:05.674 --> 00:06:06.608
It's outside the domain.

00:06:08.197 --> 00:06:08.581
Last one.

00:06:09.326 --> 00:06:11.128
For h of x equals e to the

00:06:11.309 --> 00:06:14.152
x, minus two x, for x from zero

00:06:14.213 --> 00:06:14.793
to two,

00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:18.399
find the exact global maximum and minimum values.

00:06:19.183 --> 00:06:20.110
Pause here to answer.

00:06:30.941 --> 00:06:32.564
h prime of x is e to the

00:06:32.704 --> 00:06:33.526
x minus two,

00:06:34.167 --> 00:06:36.091
zero when x is the natural log of

00:06:36.151 --> 00:06:37.473
two, inside the interval.

00:06:38.577 --> 00:06:39.506
h of zero is one.

00:06:40.568 --> 00:06:42.152
h of the natural log of two is

00:06:42.312 --> 00:06:44.317
two, minus two times the natural log of

00:06:44.377 --> 00:06:46.021
two, about point six one.

00:06:47.145 --> 00:06:49.535
h of two is e squared minus four,

00:06:49.977 --> 00:06:51.162
about three point three nine.

00:06:51.865 --> 00:06:54.035
So the maximum is at x equals two,

00:06:54.478 --> 00:06:56.065
and the minimum is at the natural log

00:06:56.086 --> 00:06:56.386
of two.

00:06:57.110 --> 00:06:59.198
Zero is h prime there, not h.

00:07:00.941 --> 00:07:03.869
So on a closed interval, list every candidate

00:07:04.109 --> 00:07:05.473
and compare the values of f.

00:07:06.094 --> 00:07:08.416
That completes this run of differential calculus,

00:07:08.956 --> 00:07:10.678
from the derivative of e to the x,

00:07:11.138 --> 00:07:13.260
all the way to global maxima and minima.

00:07:14.042 --> 00:07:16.175
Later in the course, the function comes from

00:07:16.215 --> 00:07:17.241
a real situation.

00:07:17.946 --> 00:07:20.397
Forty metres of fencing make three sides of

00:07:20.438 --> 00:07:21.984
a rectangle, against a wall.

00:07:22.649 --> 00:07:24.557
What shape gives the largest area?

00:07:27.468 --> 00:07:28.270
Here are the key notes.

00:07:28.914 --> 00:07:30.641
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:30.703 --> 00:07:31.184
to copy them.
